---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Can OCR read handwriting?",
    a: "Standard OCR is designed for printed and typed text and does poorly on handwriting. Handwritten text recognition (HTR) is a separate kind of model trained on handwriting. It works far better, but results on old cursive vary a lot with the hand, the ink, and the condition of the page.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I convert cursive to text?",
    a: "For a few pages, phone apps and general handwriting-to-text tools are worth trying. For historical records in volume, use a dedicated handwritten text recognition platform, a human transcription effort, or a service that combines recognition with human review — and check samples before committing.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the best OCR for old handwritten documents?",
    a: "There's no single answer — it depends on the script, language, era and condition. Dedicated HTR platforms such as Transkribus are built for historical documents. Test any tool on a representative sample of your own pages, including your worst ones.",
  },
  {
    q: "How does Boxchive handle handwritten pages?",
    a: "Handwritten, faded and damaged pages are flagged and human-reviewed before they land in your archive, and the archive records which pages were transcribed confidently and which need a second look.",
  },
];
---
<ContentLayout
  title="Handwriting OCR for Old Records: Converting Cursive to Text | Boxchive"
  description="Why ordinary OCR fails on handwriting, how handwritten text recognition differs, your options for old cursive records, and how to photograph pages for the best results."
  path="/guides/handwriting-ocr/"
  h1="Handwriting OCR for old records"
  eyebrow="Guide"
  lede="Ordinary OCR was built to read print. Handwriting needs a different technology — handwritten text recognition — and even that struggles with century-old cursive, faded ink and damaged pages. Here's what works, what doesn't, and how to avoid a searchable archive that silently skips your registers."
  crumbs={[{ name: "Guides", path: "/guides/" }]}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={["/searchable-archive/", "/guides/digitizing-historical-documents/", "/for/historical-societies/"]}
>
  <h2>OCR vs. handwritten text recognition</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>OCR</th><th>Handwritten text recognition (HTR)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Built for</strong></td><td>Printed and typed characters</td><td>Connected, variable handwriting</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Works well on</strong></td><td>Typed minutes, printed newsletters, forms</td><td>Consistent, legible hands; scripts it was trained on</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Struggles with</strong></td><td>Any handwriting; faint carbons</td><td>Unusual scripts, abbreviations, faded ink, crowded layouts</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    If a tool calls itself "handwriting OCR," it's usually HTR under the hood. The label matters
    less than how it does on <em>your</em> pages.
  </p>

  <h2>Why old handwriting is hard</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scripts change.</strong> Nineteenth-century copperplate, clerks' hands, and
      twentieth-century cursive look very different, and letterforms that are obvious to a human
      reader confuse models.</li>
    <li><strong>Abbreviations and conventions.</strong> Registers and ledgers are full of
      shorthand: "do." for ditto, abbreviated names and months, superscripts.</li>
    <li><strong>Ink and paper.</strong> Faded iron-gall ink, bleed-through, foxing, and water
      damage remove the contrast recognition depends on.</li>
    <li><strong>Layout.</strong> Ruled ledger columns, marginal notes, and entries written across
      each other break the reading order.</li>
    <li><strong>Many hands.</strong> A register kept for sixty years may have dozens of writers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your options</h2>
  <h3>Phone apps and general handwriting-to-text tools</h3>
  <p>
    Fine for a handful of modern, legible pages. Rarely enough for historical volumes: results
    vary, and most give no indication of which words they guessed.
  </p>
  <h3>Dedicated handwritten text recognition platforms</h3>
  <p>
    Platforms such as Transkribus are built for historical documents and can be trained on a
    specific hand. They're powerful and suit projects with someone willing to learn the tool and
    correct output.
  </p>
  <h3>Human transcription</h3>
  <p>
    Volunteers or paid transcribers remain the most accurate route for difficult material, and
    many historical societies run transcription projects. It's slow, and it needs consistent rules
    for abbreviations and uncertain readings.
  </p>
  <h3>A service with recognition plus review</h3>
  <p>
    The middle path: machine recognition on everything, human review on the pages that need it,
    and a record of which is which. That's how Boxchive works.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting the best results from photos</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light evenly.</strong> Diffuse daylight or two lamps at angles; no flash glare.</li>
    <li><strong>Hold the phone parallel</strong> to the page so lines aren't skewed.</li>
    <li><strong>Fill the frame</strong> with one page, and include the edges so nothing is cut off.</li>
    <li><strong>Focus on the writing</strong>, and retake anything blurry — recognition can't recover
      detail the photo didn't capture.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep page order</strong> consistent so entries that run across pages stay together.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Don't let search silently skip handwritten pages</h2>
  <p>
    The real risk isn't a typo in a transcription. It's a search that returns nothing because a
    register was never read — which looks exactly like "we don't have that." Whatever tool you use,
    keep track of which pages were transcribed confidently, which were partial, and which weren't
    read at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Boxchive flags handwritten, faded and damaged pages for human review, and your archive records
    what was confidently transcribed and what needs a second pair of eyes. More on how this fits
    into search: <a href="/searchable-archive/">what makes an archive searchable</a>.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
